 "gaps": [
  "CALLER_GRADE seals omit the +0.25 first-caller bonus: 1051 of the winning grades checked were first-caller wins, so board race totals exceed the sum of sealed race_points by $262.75 of points (recomputed 1784.40 vs sealed 1521.65). The bonus IS recomputable from CALLER_CALL timestamps \u2014 but not from the grade seals alone, and Ashen's minute-precision seconds_in makes some first-calls order-dependent.",
  "Two caller populations exist and are not the same number: CALLER_GRADE seals cover non-counted calls too (Terry's risky \"call\" category and Ashen's non-counted captures), and 630 matches carry a second, superseding grade seal. The earlier auto-open evaluation read ALL grades (Terry 1,632 graded / Ashen 532); the contest law population is counted-only (1519 grades on counted calls). This board keys off the counted population and prints both counts.",
  "PLAIN / EARLY headline accuracy is UNPRICED: their PREDICT seals carry model chance and the spot path, never the market price. Priced lines exist only for the $5-packet signal subsets (168 and 406 calls). The other graded calls cannot be priced from sealed data \u2014 stated, not smoothed.",
  "Caller calls are UNPRICED: 0 of 1546 counted CALLER_CALLs carry any call-time price field. No caller can pass the priced test until ingest seals the book price with the call.",
  "The contest board's house rows are built from gamelog.json / finaldata.json feeds (gamelog retains ~90 entries), not from the ledger \u2014 the population under a house total shifts as the feed rotates. This feed's house rows are ledger-wide.",
  "Two different \"points\" share one word: gamelog book points (stake P&L inside the sim bank) and contest race points (the band law). They are labeled apart here and must stay apart.",
